            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of hydroxyapatite- and porous-coated stems in total hip replacement.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>259-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>24 patients underwent bilateral hip replacement, using the SROM modular system (Depuy Johnson &amp; Johnson, MA, USA) with the porous-coated sleeve in one hip and an HA-coated sleeve in the other. 20 patients were followed for at least 4 years with clinical and radiographic evaluations. We found no difference between the two sides as regards the time of disappearance of thigh pain and hip scores. Radiographs showed a buttress sign in 18 HA-coated and 15 porous coated stems. There were no differences in the serial bone-remodeling pattern around the proximal sleeve and measurement of wear volume between the two groups. In the short- to medium-term, our findings support the view that the hydroxyapatite-coated femoral stem has no advantages over those with porous coating.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
